    "finish_elapsed_s": 9.756230731029063,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, it evaporates and absorbs heat from your food, making the inside cold. Then a compressor squeezes the refrigerant, which makes it hot. That heat travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge and escapes into your kitchen. Finally, the refrigerant cools down and turns back into liquid, ready to repeat the cycle. So a fridge doesn’t “make cold”—it removes heat, over and over, keeping your snacks chilly."
